    "summary": "Legendary Dark Star > Other One marathon with soaring solos and tight rhythm",
    "blurb": "The show delivers an epic Dark Star → The Other One suite highlighted by Jerry's monster leads and Phil’s bass bomb. First‑set energy stays high despite some early tape murkiness, and the Turn On Your Lovelight/road medley caps a rousing second set.",
    "review": "What unfolds at Bickershaw Festival on May 7, 1972, is one of the band’s most celebrated moments—a soaring Dark Star that stretches into an even longer The Other One, each section fueled by Jerry Garcia’s incandescent guitar and Phil Lesh’s thunderous bass. The first set, while occasionally hampered by tuning slips and audience‑recorded haze, still packs a punch with Mr. Charlie’s fiery solo, tight playing on Truckin' and Sugaree, and a crisp transition from China Cat Sunflower into I Know You Rider. In the second set the band locks into an explosive groove, launching into the monumental jam suite before surfacing into a high‑energy Turn On Your Lovelight/Going Down The Road Feeling Bad medley and a joyous encore of One More Saturday Night. Keith Godchaux’s organ fills sparkle throughout, especially on Good Lovin' and the Lovelight jam, while Bill Kreutzmann delivers an unforgettable birthday solo during The Other One. All told, this performance captures the Dead at a creative peak, offering both historic significance and exhilarating musicianship.",
